/-! Guidance from the project lead (the requester):

# GAP-A (EscapeShort) — lead's design. n = 3 is COMPLETE (below, from the design pass, reviewed by the lead);
# n ≥ 4 is open (not your job). Vocabulary: Submission/Mostow/HLDefs.lean. Full design text follows the summary.
## frameThinFar (general n): MultThickProp with N = N(δ,η) + pigeonhole in the compact set {g : hdist z (g z) ≤ δ}
   (conjugate z to 0 by a boost) ⇒ outside Γ•K some γ ≠ 1 is NearIdAt z η. (Same proof as frameThinDeep, uniform in z.)
## finite_of_nilpotent_fg_torsion: pure group theory — induct on the lower/upper central series; each layer is an abelian,
   finitely generated torsion group, hence finite (Mathlib: check `Group.IsNilpotent`, `CommGroup` fg torsion finite,
   `Subgroup.FG`, `IsTorsion`). Quotients of FG are FG; subgroups of FG nilpotent groups are FG (Mathlib?).
## gapA_three: follow S1–S11 of the design text below (n = 3; torsion allowed). Key steps: near-identity elliptic ⇒
   rotation about a geodesic axis; axis pointwise-stabiliser cyclic of order M ≥ c/η; ConjCount (conjugates of b₀
   near-identity at the same point are b₀^{±1}: Zassenhaus shrinking + S2 finiteness + Bieberbach commutator lemma,
   bieberbach.lean mOf_commutator); unfolding ⇒ tube inequality; centraliser acts on the axis by translations; tube
   volume ℓ₀·(π/M)·sinh²ρ ≤ 2V ⇒ ℓ₀ ≲ V/(η₁²M); screw g·b₀^j of infinite order is ε-short for η := ε/(2C'(V/η₁²+1)).
   Dispatch S3–S10 as children at once.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
I found an airtight route for n = 3 (all torsion allowed) but not for n ≥ 4. For n ≥ 4 I have a reduction to two precisely stated gaps, below.

## 1. Verdict and route

**Garland–Raghunathan does prove GAP-A**, since it gives finitely many cusps and a Siegel fundamental domain with torsion allowed. I could not reach the text (PNAS and PMC were blocked). Everything I could see says it is a ~48-page Lie-theoretic reduction theory that relies on the Kazhdan–Margulis unipotent-existence theorem, so it is not a shortcut to formalise. It stays a citation only.

Two other literature routes do not reach GAP-A:
- **Kazhdan–Margulis applied to conjugates.** The Chabauty limit of a_k⁻¹Γa_k at far-out points loses exactly the information we need. An elliptic tube seen from distance ≈ log M converges to a one-parameter parabolic group, the same limit a genuine cusp gives.
- **Gelander's Morse argument.** It gives finite generation, but bounded torsion still needs Selberg or number theory on top.

**Local arguments cannot work.** Lattices in rank-one groups over non-archimedean fields (Lubotzky, tree lattices) have unbounded torsion, and GAP-A fails there. So any proof must use archimedean volume growth of tubes. The lead's heuristic has the right mechanism. What it lacks is precise invariance, and the new lemma below supplies it.

**New key lemma (ConjCount).** Take b ∈ Γ elliptic with small rotation, and T_b := {w | NearIdAt b w η₁}.
- Any two Γ-conjugates of b that are near the identity at the same w generate a group that is nilpotent (Zassenhaus). It is generated by finite-order elements, so it is finite, so it fixes a point.
- Near-identity elements of a finite group commute. So these conjugates lie in one torus and have b's eigen-angles. There are at most C(n) = 2^⌊n/2⌋·⌊n/2⌋! of them; for n = 3 the bound is 2 (only b and b⁻¹).
- Therefore {γ | γw ∈ T_b} is a union of at most C(n) left cosets of C := C_Γ(b).

Unfolding then gives the **tube inequality**: vol(T_b ∩ D_C) ≤ C(n)·vol D = C(n)·V, where D_C is a fundamental domain of C. This is precise invariance up to bounded multiplicity, with the centraliser as the stabiliser.

**n = 3 proof (complete).** Let z be far out.
1. By FrameThinFar, some γ ≠ 1 is η-near-identity at z. If γ has infinite order it is η-short, and we are done because η ≤ ε. Otherwise call it f; it is a rotation about an axis X.
2. The pointwise stabiliser R of X has an orientation-preserving part that is cyclic of order M, with generator b₀ of angle 2π/M.
   - Because f is near-identity, 2π/M ≤ Cη, so M is large.
   - Let r = d(z, X). Since f moves z by less than η: e^r ≤ C·η·M.
3. T_{b₀} contains the tube of radius ρ around X, where e^ρ ≥ c·η₁·M.
4. The centraliser C preserves X and acts on it by translations, with kernel R⁺ (reflections and axis flips conjugate b₀ to b₀⁻¹, so they are not in C).
   - If C has no translation, the tube modulo C has infinite volume. This contradicts the tube inequality.
   - Otherwise, let ℓ₀ be the minimal translation. The tube modulo C has volume ℓ₀·(π/M)·sinh²ρ ≤ 2V, so ℓ₀ ≤ C·V/(η₁²·M).
5. Take the generator g of translation ℓ₀ and form g' = g·b₀^j with rotation angle ≤ π/M. It has infinite order and d(z, g'z) ≤ C(ℓ₀ + 1/M)·e^r ≤ C'(V/η₁² + 1)·η.
6. Choose η := ε / (2C'(V/η₁² + 1)). Then g' is ε-short at z. Such an η exists because it may depend on V, i.e. on this particular Γ.

Torsion is used only through the finite-order branch in step 1. This proof uses no Jordan theorem, no Selberg, no finite generation, and no finiteness of cusp classes. It is consistent with orbifold Dehn fillings: there ℓ₀·M = O(V), which is exactly what the bound allows.

**n ≥ 4 (same mechanism, not closed).** Let F := ⟨𝒩_η(z)⟩, which is finite and abelian. Let X := Fix F, P := Γ_X (the pointwise stabiliser), and A_P := the subgroup of P generated by its near-identity elements (abelian, index ≤ J(n)). Let b := the element of A_P∖1 of minimal angle θ_b ≤ Cη. Write k := dim Fix b and r := rank of the normal torus.

The tube inequality gives vol(Fix b / C̄)·(η₁/θ_b)^(n−1) ≤ C·V·θ_b^(−r), with r < n−1. This means:
- **k = 0:** contradiction once θ_b is small (that is, for η small).
- **k ≥ 2:** contradiction if the covolume of the stabiliser lattice on the stratum has a uniform lower bound (Kazhdan–Margulis in dimension k). For even n every stratum has even dimension, and that lower bound should follow by induction with the same lemmas. I sketched this but did not verify it.
- **k = 1:** handled only for rank 1, which is the n = 3 argument.

## 2. Lemmas

Common hypotheses: hD : IsDiscrete (SetLike.coe Γ), hL : HypLattice Γ, with D its fundamental domain and V := volH n D.

Helper definitions:
```
geodSet a b := {u | Real.exp (busemann a u + busemann b u) = (1 - inner ℝ a b) / 2}
distGeod a b u := Real.arcosh (Real.sqrt (2 * Real.exp (busemann a u + busemann b u) / (1 - inner ℝ a b)))
Tb b η₁ := {w | NearIdAt b w η₁}
```
The first uses reverse Cauchy–Schwarz for two null vectors: ⟨U,A⟩⟨U,B⟩ ≥ ⟨A,B⟩/2, with equality exactly on the geodesic.

**S1 FrameThinFar** — certain, dispatch now (this is K1/MultThick plus pigeonhole).
`∀ η > 0, ∃ K, IsCompact K ∧ ∀ z, (∀ γ : Γ, (γ:PO n 1) • z ∉ K) → ∃ γ : Γ, γ ≠ 1 ∧ NearIdAt γ z η`
Sketch: multiplicity greater than N(δ,η) forces two δ-short elements in one cell of the compact displacement set; their quotient is NearIdAt.

**S2 finite_of_nilpotent_fg_torsion** — certain, dispatch now (pure group theory; check Mathlib first).
`∀ H [Group H], Group.IsNilpotent H → Group.FG H → (∀ h : H, IsOfFinOrder h) → Finite H`
Sketch: induct on the lower central series; each abelian layer is finitely generated and torsion, hence finite.

**S3 ell_axis3** — certain, dispatch now.
`n = 3 → f ≠ 1 → IsOfFinOrder f → NearIdAt f z η → η ≤ η₀ → ∃ a b, a ∈ Sph 3 ∧ b ∈ Sph 3 ∧ a ≠ b ∧ {u | f • u = u} = geodSet a b`
Also give the displacement formula for a rotation by φ about geodSet a b:
`sinh (hdist u (f•u) / 2) = sinh (distGeod a b u) * |sin (φ/2)|`
Sketch: a near-identity f preserves orientation, and a fixed point exists (L1). Its fixed set is the hyperboloid ∩ ker(f−1), which is 2-dimensional.

**S4 axisStabCyclic3** — certain, dispatch now.
`n = 3 → IsDiscrete Γ → a ≠ b → ∃ (M : ℕ) (b₀ : Γ), 0 < M ∧ (∀ u ∈ geodSet a b, b₀ • u = u) ∧ angle b₀ = 2π/M ∧ ∀ g : Γ, (∀ u ∈ geodSet a b, g • u = u) → (g ∈ Subgroup.zpowers b₀ ∨ g reverses orientation)`
Sketch: this is a finite subgroup of O(2) acting on the normal plane.

**S5 depth3** — certain, dispatch now (depends on S3, S4).
`f ∈ zpowers b₀ → f ≠ 1 → hdist z (f•z) < η → Real.sinh (distGeod a b z) * Real.sin (π/M) ≤ η/2`
and `NearIdAt f z η → 2π/M ≤ C₀ * η`.

**S6 conjCount3 (ConjCount for n = 3)** — certain, dispatch now (depends on S2, Zassenhaus, S3, Bieberbach commutator).
`M ≥ M₀ → NearIdAt b₀ w η₁ → ∀ γ : Γ, NearIdAt (γ⁻¹*b₀*γ) w η₁ → γ⁻¹*b₀*γ = b₀ ∨ γ⁻¹*b₀*γ = b₀⁻¹`
Sketch:
- b₀ and b' := γ⁻¹b₀γ generate a nilpotent group (Zassenhaus). It is torsion-generated, so finite by S2, and it fixes a point p.
- Iterated commutators [b₀,[b₀,…b']] shrink (Bieberbach m([A,B]) ≤ 2m(A)m(B)). In a finite group they are therefore eventually 1.
- The Frobenius step: if b₀ commutes with [b₀,b'], then b' preserves axis(b₀). A flip would not be near-identity, so the axes coincide. The angles are equal, so b' = b₀^(±1).

**S7 unfoldTube** — certain, dispatch now (measure theory; reuse the MultThick unfolding identity).
`IsFundamentalDomain Γ D volH → IsFundamentalDomain (Subgroup.centralizer {b₀} ⊓ Γ) DC volH → (∀ w ∈ Tb b₀ η₁, ∀ γ, (γ•w ∈ Tb b₀ η₁ → γ ∈ C*γ₁(w) ∪ C*γ₂(w))) → volH (Tb b₀ η₁ ∩ DC) ≤ 2 * volH D`
Sketch: ∫_D #{γ | γw ∈ T ∩ D_C} ≤ 2·vol D, because each coset meets D_C at most once for almost every w.

**S8 centraliserTranslations3** — certain, dispatch now (depends on pairStab, S4).
`C := centralizer b₀ in Γ; (∀ c ∈ C, bd c a = a ∧ bd c b = b) ∧ ((∀ c ∈ C, busemann a (c•0) = 0) ∨ ∃ g ∈ C, 0 < |busemann a (g•0)| ∧ ∀ c ∈ C, ∃ k : ℤ, busemann a (c•0) = k * busemann a (g•0))`
Sketch: C preserves Fix b₀, and discreteness makes the translations a lattice in ℝ.

**S9 tubeVolume3** — certain but analytic (Fermi coordinates); dispatch now.
Let ρ be the tube radius with 2·sinh(ρ+1)·sin(π/M) ≤ η₁/C.
- In the translation case: `volH (Tb b₀ η₁ ∩ DC) ≥ ℓ₀ * (π/M) * Real.sinh ρ ^ 2 / 2`
- In the no-translation case: `volH (Tb b₀ η₁ ∩ DC) = ⊤`

Sketch: the volume element is sinh s·cosh s ds dθ dt. Take the region {distGeod ≤ ρ, busemann-coordinate ∈ [0, ℓ₀), angle ∈ [0, 2π/M)} inside T; it is contained in a fundamental domain of C.

**S10 screwShort3** — certain, dispatch now.
`g ∈ C, translation ℓ₀ → ∃ j : ℤ, let g' := g*b₀^j; ¬ IsOfFinOrder g' ∧ hdist z (g'•z) ≤ 2*(ℓ₀ + π/M) * Real.cosh (distGeod a b z)`

**S11 gapA_three** — assembly (depends on S1–S10).
`n = 3 → HypLattice Γ → ∀ ε > 0, ∃ K, IsCompact K ∧ ∀ u, (∀ γ : Γ, γ • u ∉ K) → ∃ g : Γ, ¬ IsOfFinOrder (g:PO 3 1) ∧ hdist u (g • u) < ε`
Take η := ε/(2C'(V/η₁²+1)) and K from S1(η).

For n ≥ 4 (these depend on the gaps below):

**G1 jordanFrobenius** — certain, heavy.
`Finite H → (∀ h ∈ H, h•p = p) → NearIdAt a p θJ → NearIdAt c p θJ → a ∈ H → c ∈ H → a*c = c*a`
Plus index ≤ J(n) by pigeonhole. The Frobenius step needs eigenvalues.

**G2 conjCount** — certain; depends on G1 and a torus signed-permutation count ≤ C(n).
General-n version of S6.

**G3 tubeIneq** — certain.
vol((Fix b)/C̄)·(η₁/θ_b)^(n−1)·θ_b^r ≤ C·V.

## 3. Not needed

Selberg, finite generation, Garland–Raghunathan, the Chabauty or invariant-random-subgroup machinery, bounded torsion (K0), GAP-D, finiteness of cusp classes, Jordan's theorem (for n = 3), MultThick beyond S1, and the cusp chapter L0–L12.

## 4. Remaining gap for n ≥ 4

- **StratumCovol_k (2 ≤ k ≤ n−2).** A uniform lower bound v_k on vol(Y/C̄) when C̄ is a lattice on a k-dimensional fixed stratum Y. This is Kazhdan–Margulis in dimension k. For even k I sketched an induction: every lattice has a point that is either thick or of finite type, because an all-infinite-type lattice is elementary by connectedness, and the finite-type case then uses G3 with lower even strata. I did not verify it. Formalising it also needs sub-hyperbolic-space vocabulary, which the workspace does not have.
- **GeodStratumRank≥2 (odd n ≥ 5).** Fix b is a geodesic and the normal torus has rank ≥ 2. The generic case works: pigeonhole over g₀^j·p in the torus gives vol ≳ M^(1/3)·ℓ^(−1/3). Degenerate cases do not yet: the rotation of g₀ does not centralise A_P, or there are equal-angle blocks.

The best reduction for n ≥ 4: GAP-A follows from G1–G3 together with these two statements.

Recommendation: dispatch S1–S11 now; that closes GAP-A for n = 3. Then decide whether Mostow may cite Kazhdan–Margulis, i.e. whether to fund StratumCovol and GeodStratumRank≥2 as separate crux passes.

Sources: [Gelander, Lectures on lattices](https://www.math.utah.edu/pcmi12/lecture_notes/gelander.pdf), [Gelander, KM for IRS, arXiv 1510.05423](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1510.05423), [Garland–Raghunathan PNAS announcement](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.62.2.309) (not reachable), [Baumgartner/Lubotzky rank-one over local fields, arXiv math/0302111](https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0302111), [Kazhdan–Margulis theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kazhdan%E2%80%93Margulis_theorem), [Non-divergence in rank one, arXiv 1408.2591](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1408.2591) (assumes virtually torsion-free).
-/


